# Copy to `.env` for local development.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ means this is inlined into the JavaScript bundle at BUILD time,
# not read at runtime. Two consequences worth knowing before deploying:
#
#   1. It must be present when `next build` runs. On Railway it's a normal
#      service variable, which is available during the build — but a value
#      changed after a build has no effect until the service is rebuilt, not
#      merely restarted.
#
#   2. It ships to the browser. Only ever put public values behind this prefix;
#      nothing here is a secret.

# Origin of the Hono API, no trailing slash.
# On Railway, reference the server service rather than hardcoding its URL:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL=https://${{server.RAILWAY_PUBLIC_DOMAIN}}
NEXT_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:4000

# OPTIONAL. Set this to the same value as the server's COOKIE_DOMAIN — and only
# when that is set — e.g. ".example.com".
#
# It tells proxy.ts whether the session cookie is scoped to a domain this host
# shares with the API, and therefore whether the middleware can see it at all.
# The API sets the cookie on its own host, so on unrelated hosts (Railway's
# default *.up.railway.app domains) it never reaches this app, and the
# middleware must not gate navigation on it — it would redirect every request
# to the login page, including one from a user who just signed in.
#
# Leave it unset and route protection falls to RequireSession, which asks the
# API directly. Correct either way; setting it only buys a faster redirect.
#
# Leave unset locally: the middleware check happens to work on localhost
# because cookies ignore ports, but matching production is more useful here.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.example.com
